// `Result<T>` is universal (D-013), and a return type names the SUCCESS type.

// D-091 — writing `Result<T>` as a return type is an error that says so. It means
// either the same type there are now two ways to write, or a nesting nobody
// wanted that every caller unwraps twice; both readings are worse than refusing
// the spelling.
//
// ONE DIAGNOSTIC, NOT TWO. The spelling resolves perfectly well — that is the
// problem with it — so a checker that reported the declaration and then every
// `pass` inside it would say the same thing twice. The signature yields the
// invalid type instead.
// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-010
// expect-error-at: 14:23
func:double_wrapped = Result<int32>() never fails {
    pass 1i32;
};

// A well-formed function, so the case below is about the CALL rather than about
// this declaration.
func:width = int32() never fails {
    pass 4i32;
};

// A call yields a `Result` and using it as a value is a mistake — which is what
// makes the discipline visible at every call site rather than at a boundary
// somebody has to remember.
// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-007
func:unwrapped_call = int32() never fails {
    int32:v = width();
    pass v;
};

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) { pass 0i32; };
func:failsafe = int32(Error:_~e) { exit 1i32; };
